From 49eb1427f555bbc8720dcca93708168a5cc7e2e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Caleb E." <158400752+FoxesRCool1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 15:30:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] dspqueue: async Queue Monitor pump and exit cancel

With DSPQUEUE_QUEUE_MONITOR=1 (previous patch) every dspqueue_write
waits inside the method 9 invoke until the DSP-side packet callback
returns. ggml-hexagon's callback keeps polling the ring for about 10 ms
after the last packet, so each request/response round trip costs that
idle tail: about 81 tokens/s on a 15M model, matching the numbers in
qualcomm/fastrpc#377.

Add DSPQUEUE_QUEUE_MONITOR=2: each queue gets a pump thread that issues
the method 9 invoke, and dspqueue_write only flags it. While a callback
is running on the DSP it consumes later packets itself, and the host can
read responses as soon as the DSP writes them.

Guards:
- a thread that wrote to a pumped queue waits (2 s at most) for the
  pumps of that domain to go idle before it makes any other
  remote_handle64_invoke, so a client's own "stop" method cannot close
  the queue on the DSP under a running monitor invoke;
- dspqueue_close() stops and joins the queue's pump thread;
- an atexit handler (registered on the first pump, mode 1 or 2) waits
  up to 500 ms for idle pumps, then cancels and joins the receive-signal
  thread's wait_signal invoke. Clients such as llama.cpp exit without
  closing their queues; exit_group() then interrupts that invoke, and on
  mainline fastrpc.c an interrupted invoke stays on fl->pending and keeps
  the process's DSP mappings until reboot. A later dspqueue_close() does
  not cancel or join that thread a second time.

Without DSPQUEUE_QUEUE_MONITOR=1 or 2 nothing changes: no thread, no
atexit handler, and the invoke gate returns at once.
